[{:seon.task/name "two-values-of-one-type-that-cannot-be-ordered-are-never-taken-for-equal"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes). Upstream Datahike's safe-compare rethrows when compare throws for two values of the SAME type (datom.cljc near 535 upstream), because answering an order by type name there made two different values equal and an index silently dropped one. Our fork's safe-compare still answers the type-name order, which is 0 for one type. Read on our pin (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/datom.cljc): writes are not affected: an insert compares through compare-value (the prefix and quick comparators), which on Jolt orders scalars by compare, byte arrays element-wise and plain data (maps, sets, sequences) by compare-data, and for anything else rethrows when both values have one type, so such a write fails with the exception, it is not deduplicated. Only reads are: a slice's comparator goes through cmp-nil to safe-compare, so a read bounded by a value of a type compare cannot order answers every datom of that entity and attribute holding a value of the same type, a wrong answer, never a lost write. Whether Seon can reach it: by reading, no: every value type Seon declares is one compare-value orders (strings, numbers, keywords, symbols, uuids, booleans, bytes, plain data); not run: whether Jolt's compare orders two instants and two chars, which compare-value sends to compare without a scalar-kind arm. So it rides the Datahike upstream merge: take upstream's rethrow in safe-compare, and make the refusal a typed error. Proof: a read bounded by one of two same-type values compare cannot order answers a typed error, not the other value's datom; and two instants and two chars order (the two not run today)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
